{"id":1325,"date":"2020-11-26T17:29:31","date_gmt":"2020-11-27T01:29:31","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1325"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:40:48","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:40:48","slug":"rokit-variables","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/rokit-variables\/","title":{"rendered":"Variables"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22160282cdf\" name=\"6a22160282cdf\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Briefing video<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Variables are found in almost every program that you will come across, including the ones you will be making for your Rokit Smart bots. One of our instructors explains what they are in this video![\/vc_column_text][vc_video link=&#8221;https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/watch?v=epU53qLhr8s&#8221; align=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22160282de8\" name=\"6a22160282de8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are variables?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Variables are a way to store information in a program. The easiest way to think of them is as if they were different sized boxes. In these boxes, you can store things and then write a name on the box to help you remember what is inside. For example, one box could be named kitchen and have plates in it, while another box could be named books and have books in it.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22160282e68\" name=\"6a22160282e68\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Types of variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]This\u00a0<em>variable<\/em>\u00a0is named &#8220;values&#8221; and stores the number 6:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int values = 6;<\/pre>\n<p>This variable is named \u201cletters\u201d and stores the letter &#8216;S&#8217;:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">char letters = 'S';<\/pre>\n<p>You might be asking, &#8220;But Robolink! Why do you have &#8220;int&#8221; and &#8220;char&#8221;? Why can&#8217;t you just use one of them?&#8221; The first word tells you what type of information will be stored in the\u00a0<em>variable<\/em>, and there are three main types of\u00a0<em>variables<\/em>\u00a0you will be using:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>\u00a0 \u00a0 int: numbers from\u00a0-32,768 to 32,767<\/li>\n<li>\u00a0 \u00a0 char: letters (actually numbers that are represented by letters, just like algebra!)<\/li>\n<li>\u00a0 \u00a0 byte:\u00a0numbers from\u00a0-128 to 127<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22160282ebf\" name=\"6a22160282ebf\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Correct syntax for variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In order to make your life easier, you only need to have the data type labeled when you create the variable. For example:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int values = 6;\r\nvalues = 4;<\/pre>\n<p>The label int isn&#8217;t needed the second time around because the variable was already created.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22160282f2c\" name=\"6a22160282f2c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Setting variables equal to something<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Just like math, variables can be set to other variables:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int a = 1;\r\nint b = a;<\/pre>\n<p>But unlike math, variables can be set to themselves:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int values = 6;\r\nvalues = values + 2;<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>This is because the equal sign in programming doesn&#8217;t necessarily mean &#8220;is equal to&#8221;. If the equal sign is by itself, like in the examples, it means &#8220;gets the value&#8221;. In the example above, the computer would see that the values variable gets the value of 6, and then it would get the value of 6 + 2.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__question\">\n<div class=\"styled-checkbox activity2__button-checkbox\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22160282fa0\" name=\"6a22160282fa0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Example code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Below is an example of a program that uses many variables:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">\/\/ create the variables\r\nint A = 1;\r\nint B = 2;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n    Serial.begin(9600); \/\/ start the Serial Monitor\r\n}\r\n \r\nvoid loop() {\r\n    A = A + 1; \/\/ add one to A\r\n    \/\/ print out the value of A\r\n    Serial.print(\"A = \"); \/\/ prints: A = \r\n    Serial.println(A); \/\/ prints: the value of A\r\n\r\n    B = A \/ 2; \/\/ set B to half of A\r\n    \/\/ print out the value of B\r\n    Serial.print(\"B = \"); \/\/ prints: B = \r\n    Serial.println(B); \/\/ prints: the value of B\r\n    \r\n    \/\/ delay to slow it down\r\n    delay(100);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is what&#8217;s going on! At the top of the code, variables A and B were created.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Communication with the serial monitor was started at a baud rate of 9600 in void setup().<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>There&#8217;s a lot going on inside void loop():<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>First, 1 is added to A through the loop.<\/li>\n<li>Then, the value of A is printed in the serial monitor.<\/li>\n<li>Next, B is set to half of the value of A.<\/li>\n<li>After that, the value of B is printed on the serial monitor.<\/li>\n<li>Finally, a short delay of 0.1 seconds, or 100 milliseconds, slows down the loop enough so that you can see what is happening.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>If you ran this program as is and watched the serial monitor, you would see A&#8217;s value going up by 1 every time, while B&#8217;s value would always remain at half of A&#8217;s value.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 Briefing video [vc_column_text]Variables are found in almost every program that you will come across, including the ones you will be making for your Rokit Smart bots. One&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/rokit-variables\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1735,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":802,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1760,1737,626,1533,1438,144,209],"lesson-tag":[2163,2123,2186,2122,2171,2117],"class_list":["post-1325","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-mouse","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-rokit-smart","lesson-tag-variables","module-mouse","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1325","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":3,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1325\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1329,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1325\/revisions\/1329"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1735"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1325"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1325"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1325"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}